.TH WALL 10 "October 2026" "1.1" "Neverdark Manual"
.SH NAME
wall \- Shout to everyone in a region
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wall
.RI [ region ]
.I message
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Everyone who can see you shouting, for every listener standing in the region
you selected and in every subregion under it. Only a player who rules the
selected region, or an ancestor of it, may shout to it; anyone else is refused
and told who rules it.
.PP
The region is a bare world number (0\-65535) or the keyword \fBcosmos\fP \fB under
exactly the same rules as every other region selector \fBban\fP, \fBmodlist\fP,
\fBdeny\fP and the rest. Note that \fBworld 0\fP is \fBnot\fP the cosmos: world 0
is the first subregion of the cosmos, and only the \fBcosmos\fP keyword names the
root. If no region is given, the command uses your \fBdefault target region\fP
(see \fBtarget\fP(10)), or the region you are standing in if you have set none.
.PP
.SS The leading token
.PP
A leading word is treated as a region selector only if it parses as one
\fBand\fP something follows it. This command is the odd one out among the region
commands: its first argument is also the first word of its payload. So:
.PP
.RS
.nf
wall 3                the message "3"
wall 3 mice are down  world 3, message "mice are down"
wall mice are down    your default target region, message "mice are down"
.fi
.RE
.PP
There is a matching misparse, and it is inherent rather than a defect:
\fBwall cosmos is down\fP is read as the selector \fBcosmos\fP plus the message
"is down", not as the single message "cosmos is down". Likewise
\fBwall 3 blind mice is down\fP. Quoting is not available, so a message may not
begin with a bare number or with the word \fBcosmos\fP unless you intend it as a
selector. Requiring an explicit keyword prefix in front of every message would
remove the misparse, at the cost of a private syntax this command no longer has.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR target,ban,unban,modlist,loadmod,unloadmod,deny,here,say,pose (10)
.SH AUTHOR
quirinpa <quirinpa@tty.pt>
